  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nFair weather continues across eastern New York and western New\nEngland through the remainder of the day and into tonight as a\nlow pressure system continues to depart to the east. Tomorrow, a\nweak shortwave will rotate through the region along the\nsouthwest periphery of the departing upper low. While \nwidespread rain is not expected as a result, there is potential\nfor isolated to scattered showers and perhaps a rumble of \nthunder or two across portions of the Southern Adirondacks, \nMohawk Valley, Eastern Catskills, and western New England. Most \nof these will occur at the highest elevations of these areas \nwhere upslope flow will overcome otherwise dry air. \n\nHigh pressure will then build in across the region Sunday\nthrough Tuesday, yielding pleasant conditions not just for the\nremainder of the Labor Day weekend, but heading into the\nbeginning of the shortened work week. Chances for rain and\npotential thunderstorms then increase Wednesday through Thursday\nas our next frontal system approaches from the north and west.\nAt this time, confidence is low in the potential for severe\nweather given uncertainties in the timing of the incoming\nsystem. However, there is high confidence at this time that\nthere will not be any flooding concerns that result from this\nsystem. Latest guidance indicates about 30% to 70% probabilities\nfor at least 0.25\" across the region with the highest of these\nacross the higher terrain of the Southern Adirondacks, Southern\nGreens, Berkshires, and Taconics. With several days of mostly\ndry weather leading up to this event, all of this rain would be\nbeneficial.",
